course: biology
lesson: 224
title: Raccoons, opossums and skunks
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In a city like Toronto, a family may wake up to find the green bin open and rubbish all over the drive: a raccoon has lifted the lid with its clever little hands, which have five fingers and are so sensitive that it can feel food it cannot see.
explain: A raccoon seems to wash its food in a stream, but really it is feeling it, because water makes its paws even more sensitive; its black mask may help it see at night by cutting glare.
explain: The opossum is a marsupial, like a kangaroo: its babies grow in a pouch and then ride on their mother's back, and when it is badly frightened it falls down, goes stiff and gives off a nasty smell, which is called playing possum.
explain: The skunk does not attack without warning: it stamps, raises its tail and only then sprays a liquid so smelly that a dog will remember it for life.
explain: And the Australian possum living in a roof is a different animal from the American opossum, though both carry their babies in pouches.
fact: A raccoon's front paws have five fingers and are very sensitive, so it can open bin lids, jars and even door latches.
fact: A raccoon dipping food in water is feeling it, not washing it; water makes its paws even more sensitive.
fact: The black mask round a raccoon's eyes may reduce glare and help it see at night, though scientists are not completely sure.
fact: Raccoons are common in North American cities like Toronto, where they raid bins and nest in attics and chimneys.
fact: Raccoons were brought to Europe and some escaped; now they live wild in Germany and are spreading to nearby countries.
fact: When badly frightened, an opossum may play dead: it falls over, stays still and gives off a bad smell; this is called playing possum.
fact: Opossums are marsupials: the tiny babies grow in their mother's pouch and later ride on her back.
fact: The Virginia opossum has fifty teeth, more than any other North American land mammal, and eats almost anything.
fact: Adult opossums cannot really hang by their tails for long like in cartoons; young ones can briefly, and the tail helps them grip branches and carry leaves.
fact: Australian possums, such as the brushtail possum that often lives in roofs, are different from American opossums; both are marsupials but from different families.
fact: Before spraying, a skunk warns by stamping its feet and raising its tail; then it sprays a very smelly liquid from glands under its tail.
fact: A raccoon, an opossum and a skunk each have four legs, one tail and a coat of fur.
exercise: How does a raccoon open a bin lid? | With clever, sensitive front paws with five fingers | By pushing it with its nose only | By jumping up and down on it until the lid breaks | By biting the handle off with its sharp front teeth
exercise: What is a raccoon really doing when it dips its food in water? | Feeling it, because water makes its paws more sensitive | Washing off the germs, just like a careful person does | Cooling it down because it is hot | Drowning the food so that it stops wriggling about
exercise: What might a raccoon's black mask help it do? | See better at night by cutting glare | Hide its face from other raccoons | Keep its eyes warm in the winter snow | Scare people, just like a robber's mask
exercise: Where do city raccoons often nest? | In attics and chimneys | In the middle of busy roads | In birds' nests on lampposts | Under the water in the lake
exercise: Why are there wild raccoons in Germany? | Raccoons brought to Europe escaped and spread | They swam across the sea from America long ago | They have always lived there | They were dropped there by big birds
exercise: What does playing possum mean? | Falling down, staying still and smelling bad to fool an attacker | Hanging upside down from a tree all day | Running round in circles to confuse a dog | Hiding its babies under leaves and running off to fool an attacker
exercise: What kind of mammal is an opossum? | A marsupial, with a pouch for its babies | A rodent, just like a rat or a mouse | A kind of monkey | A kind of wild cat
exercise: Where do opossum babies go after they leave the pouch? | They ride on their mother's back | They live alone in a hollow tree | They swim in the nearest river | They go to live with their father
exercise: How many teeth does a Virginia opossum have? | Fifty | Thirty-two | Twenty | One hundred
exercise: What does an opossum eat? | Almost anything | Only insects | Only bananas | Only fish from rivers
exercise: Can a grown-up opossum hang by its tail for hours like in cartoons? | No; young ones can briefly, and the tail helps them grip | Yes, it sleeps hanging by its tail every single night | Yes, but only from telephone wires | No, because opossums have no tails at all, only stumps
exercise: Is the Australian brushtail possum the same animal as the American opossum? | No, both have pouches but they are from different families | Yes, it is exactly the same animal | Yes, it swam all the way to Australia from America long ago | No, the brushtail possum is a kind of squirrel
exercise: What is a skunk doing when it stamps and raises its tail? | Warning before it sprays | Saying hello to a friend | Getting ready to climb a tree | Showing off to find a mate
exercise: Where does a skunk's smelly spray come from? | Glands under its tail | Its mouth when it spits | Its fur when it gets wet | Its ears when it is angry
exercise: How many legs does a raccoon have? | Four | Two | Six | Eight
test: «raccoons got into the green bin AGAIN, rubbish everywhere, how do they even open it» | Their clever five-fingered paws can lift the lid | They ring the doorbell to scare people off first | They push it over and the lid melts | They wait for the wind to open it
test: In an ice age cartoon two opossum brothers hang upside down by their tails all day. What is true? | Adult opossums cannot hang by their tails for long; young ones only briefly | Real opossums sleep hanging by their tails every night | Real opossums have no tails | Real opossums hang upside down by their ears, not their tails, all day long
test: «possum on the road, not moving, it's dead innit» What could really be going on? | It may be playing dead because it is frightened | It is definitely asleep for the whole winter | It is waiting for a car to give it a lift | It is digging a hole under the road to hide its babies
test: Count them: a mother opossum carries 7 babies on her back. How many tails are there altogether? | 8 | 7 | 14 | 1
test: In an old cartoon a skunk walks around and nobody notices the smell until he is close. What is true about real skunks? | They warn with stamping and a raised tail before they spray | They spray every animal they meet without any warning at all | They smell of perfume to attract friends | They spray only from their mouths
test: «there's a possum in our roof in Melbourne, same as the American ones?» | No, Australian possums are a different family, though both have pouches | Yes, they are the same animal | Yes, they were brought over from America on ships and let go in the bush | No, Australian possums are rodents
test: What would most likely happen if a dog chased a skunk that had already stamped and raised its tail? | The dog would get sprayed with a terrible smell | The skunk would play dead at once | The skunk would climb a tree like a cat | Nothing, skunks never spray dogs
test: A raccoon sits by a stream rubbing a crayfish, a little river animal like a tiny lobster, in the water. «aww it's washing its dinner» What is right? | It is feeling the food, since wet paws are more sensitive | Yes, it is washing off the dirt | It is trying to bring the crayfish back to life | It is cooling the food down in the cold water before it eats
test: «saw a raccoon in a German forest, did it escape from a zoo or what» | Raccoons brought to Europe escaped and now live wild there | It must have escaped from a zoo that very same day | Raccoons have always lived in Germany | It must have been a badger
test: Count them: a raccoon has 5 fingers on each front paw. How many fingers on 2 raccoons' front paws? | 20 | 10 | 40 | 25
test: What would happen to an opossum's babies if they could not fit in the pouch any more? | They would ride on their mother's back | They would be left behind at once | They would grow a pouch of their own | They would hang from her tail
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the behaviour in which a raccoon immerses its food in water. | The sensitivity of the forepaws is thereby enhanced, permitting the food to be examined by touch | The food is thereby cleansed of harmful microorganisms | The food is thereby cooled prior to consumption | The prey is thereby drowned prior to consumption, so that it may no longer resist the raccoon
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the behaviour termed playing possum? | The animal collapses, remains motionless and emits an offensive odour | The animal suspends itself by the tail for extended periods | The animal conceals its young beneath fallen leaves | The animal flees in a circular path to confuse a pursuer
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the dentition of the Virginia opossum. | It possesses fifty teeth, exceeding all other North American land mammals | It possesses thirty-two teeth, as does the adult human | It possesses no teeth and consumes only soft food | It possesses one hundred teeth, which are shed and replaced annually
exam: Ascertain the relationship between the brushtail possum of Australia and the opossum of the Americas. | Both are marsupials, but they belong to different families | They are a single species distributed across two continents | The former is a rodent, whereas the latter is a marsupial | The latter was introduced to Australia, where it became the former
